src/test/prop/README

Property-based tests
====================

This directory holds PostgreSQL's optional property-based tests (PBTs), built
on the Hegel engine (libhegel; see https://hegel.dev).  A property-based test
states a property that must hold for every input in some domain -- a
round-trip, an invariant, an algebraic identity, or agreement between two ways
of computing the same answer -- and the engine generates many inputs trying to
falsify it.  When it finds a counterexample it shrinks it to a minimal failing
input and prints a blob that replays it exactly.

These tests are entirely optional and are off by default.  They are built and
run only when all of the following hold:

1. you explicitly enable them at configure time (--with-hegel for autoconf,
   -Dprop_tests=enabled for meson);

2. libhegel (its hegel.h header and libhegel_c shared library) is present and
   reachable via --with-includes/--with-libraries (autoconf) or
   --extra-include-dirs/--extra-lib-dirs (meson); and

3. TAP tests are enabled (--enable-tap-tests / -Dtap_tests=enabled).

If any piece is missing the suite is silently skipped.  If the opt-in flag is
set but a piece is missing, configure fails with a clear message rather than
quietly doing nothing.

See DESIGN in this directory for how it all works under the covers.


When to use a property-based test (and when not to)
===================================================

Reach for a PBT when the code has a property that should hold across a large
input space:

* Round-trips: parse(format(x)) = x, decompress(compress(x)) = x.

* Idempotence: f(f(x)) = f(x) -- normalization, case folding, canonical path
  cleanup.

* Invariants and contracts: a verifier stops on a character boundary; a
  function's output stays within documented bounds; a comparator is a total
  order.

* Differential oracles: two ways of computing the same answer must agree -- an
  index scan versus a sequential scan, a query with an optimization on versus
  off, a partitioned table versus an unpartitioned one with the same rows.

* No-crash and robustness: a parser handles arbitrary bytes without crashing.

Prefer an ordinary regression or TAP test when:

* The test checks exact output.  "render(x) = <html>..." depends on a specific
  format; there is no general property, just a fixed expectation.

* The test checks a specific error message.  PBTs are good at "an error is
  raised", poor at "the error says exactly this".  Test the error class or
  SQLSTATE, never the wording (it is reworded and translated between releases).

* The output is deliberately nondeterministic: random(), unseeded
  gen_random_uuid(), clock_timestamp(), pg_stat_*, timing, memory figures.
  There is no stable oracle.

* The target is plan shape or cost, not plan results.  EXPLAIN output is
  cost-model dependent and is intended to change across releases and with
  statistics.  (Plan results are a prime PBT target -- see the query_* areas;
  plan shape is not.)

* The only oracle is a second copy of the same algorithm.  If the test
  reimplements the function to check the function, it finds bugs in the test,
  not the server.  This is the single most common way a property goes wrong;
  prefer a round-trip or a differential oracle (two independent ways that must
  agree) over an invented formula.

* The target is floating-point textual edge output (extra_float_digits,
  last-ULP rendering): that is platform and libc behavior, not PostgreSQL's.

* The target is performance or resource use.  PBT asserts correctness, not
  speed; that is a benchmark.

* Setup dominates.  If each case needs elaborate fixtures, the generator is
  mostly scaffolding and a few hand-written cases are clearer.

* No real property is apparent.  Don't force a tautology ("x = x"); a good
  unit test beats a contrived PBT.

The rule of thumb: a PBT belongs only where a disagreement is unambiguously a
bug.  That is why the SQL-oracle (Tier B) areas lean on differential oracles
and round-trips, not invented formulas.

A PBT complements, rather than replaces, the existing regression and TAP
suites.  Its value is exercising the combinations and boundary inputs a human
does not think to write by hand -- the three-byte "0.0" fed one byte at a time,
the two-partition prefix-equality query -- not raising line coverage.  Measured,
the suite adds only about 0.4% line coverage over the regression and isolation
suites, and it does not execute shared lines more often than they do; its
measurable gain is reaching different lines (for example, 242 lines of
src/common/jsonapi.c that the json and jsonb regression tests never reach) plus
the standing differential net over the planner and executor.  See DESIGN for
the measurement method and figures.


Running
=======

The tests are organized into areas, each targeting one piece of code.  There
are two kinds.  In-process (Tier A) areas link the code under test directly
into the test program and need no server.  SQL-oracle (Tier B) areas drive SQL
against a running cluster and compare the answer to an oracle.  All areas are
compiled into one program, pg_prop, whose first argument selects the area:

    pg_prop <area> [options] [property ...]

Through the test harness:

    # meson: run the whole prop suite (both TAP drivers)
    meson test --suite prop

    # meson: one driver (001 = in-process areas, 002 = SQL-oracle areas)
    meson test prop/001_prop
    meson test prop/002_sql_props

    # autoconf
    make -C src/test/prop check

Directly, for development and debugging.  An in-process area needs no server:

    pg_prop --list                               # list every area/property
    pg_prop jsonapi --list                        # any area filters the list
    pg_prop jsonapi                               # all jsonapi properties
    pg_prop jsonapi chunking_agrees_with_oneshot  # one property
    pg_prop jsonapi chunking_agrees_with_oneshot -n 100000 -s 42

A SQL-oracle area reads PGHOST/PGPORT/PGDATABASE from the environment, so point
it at any cluster:

    PGHOST=/tmp PGPORT=5432 PGDATABASE=postgres \
        pg_prop partprune pruning_preserves_rows -n 100000 -s 42

Controlling iterations and the seed:

* -n, --iterations N -- number of test cases per property (default 100, kept
  low so check-world stays cheap; raise it when hunting a bug).

* -s, --seed N -- fix the RNG seed for a reproducible run.  When you do not give
  one, pg_prop picks a seed and prints it ("# seed: NNN"), so a failure seen in
  CI can be replayed with -s NNN.

The same three controls are available as the environment variables
PG_PROP_ITERATIONS, PG_PROP_SEED, and PG_PROP_TESTS (a space-separated list of
property names), which the TAP drivers forward -- so you can raise the
iteration count of a meson test run without editing anything:

    PG_PROP_ITERATIONS=10000 PG_PROP_SEED=42 meson test --suite prop
    PG_PROP_TESTS="pruning_preserves_rows" meson test prop/002_sql_props


Active and frozen areas
=======================

The SQL-oracle driver runs the active areas by default: the query generator
and its oracles (query_*), the stateful, concurrency and access-method areas,
and the commitfest areas (cf_*).  The per-type scalar areas (numeric, text,
jsonb, ...) are frozen: they compile and run with PG_PROP_FROZEN=1, but are
not maintained.  In the 2026 evaluation they found no server bug and most of
their findings were unsound oracles.  A frozen area that breaks after an
upstream change may be deleted rather than fixed.

New work goes into the generator (sql/query_gen.c) rather than new oracles.
A new query shape is checked by every existing oracle at once; a new oracle
checks only the shapes the generator already makes.

The single-table generator (qg_build_table) and the oracles over it are
reproducible: a counterexample replays from its seed.  The deep multi-table
generator (qg_build_db / qg_build_query, used by the query_deep, query_multi
and diff_deep_* properties under PG_PROP_DEEP) is not currently reproducible
for a fixed seed -- the engine's failure-replay can build a different query
(see findings 041).  Treat a deep disagreement as a lead to reproduce by
hand, not as a replayable counterexample, and do not use a deep run as a
regression gate until that is fixed.


Commitfest properties and retirement
====================================

A cf_* property encodes one open commitfest entry: it fails on master and
passes with that entry's patch.  It earns its keep while the patch is under
review.  Once the fix is committed, the patch's own regression test covers
the case, and the property is retired: delete it (or move its invariant into
a generator shape, if it generalises).  scripts/cf-retire.sh lists the
properties whose CF entry is committed, withdrawn or rejected.


Debugging a failure
===================

1. Read the counterexample.  On failure pg_prop prints a one-line "falsified:"
   message that includes the minimal inputs Hegel shrank to, plus a "rerun
   with" blob.  The TAP drivers echo this as "#" diagnostics.

2. Reproduce it deterministically.  Re-run the single property with the seed
   from the run's "# seed:" line:

       pg_prop <area> <property> -s <seed> -n <iterations>

   (For a SQL area, set PGHOST/PGPORT first.)  You can also raise verbosity with
   -v to see every drawn value, not just the final counterexample.

3. Decide what the failure means.  There are three possibilities:

   * A real bug in the code under test -- the code violated its own contract.
     Capture the reproducer.

   * An unsound property -- the test asserted something the code never promised
     (for example, that a string verifier and a character verifier agree on a
     NUL byte -- they deliberately do not).  Fix the property.

   * An over-broad generator -- the failing input is genuinely outside the
     function's domain.  Only then narrow the generator, and say why in a
     comment.

4. Step through it.  The reproducing command is an ordinary process; run it
   under gdb or lldb with a breakpoint in the code under test.  Because the seed
   is fixed, the same inputs are generated every time.


Adding a property
=================

To add a property to an existing area, add a PG_PROP(name) { ... } function to
that area's prop_<area>.c.  It is registered automatically.

To add a new area, create prop_<area>.c beginning with

    #define PG_PROP_AREA "<area>"

before including pg_prop.h, then add the file to the source list in meson.build,
the OBJS list in Makefile, and the area list in the matching TAP driver
(t/001_prop.pl for in-process areas, t/002_sql_props.pl for SQL-oracle areas).

See pg_prop.h for the full generator and assertion API, and DESIGN for the
rationale and internals.


Extensions
==========

An extension can take part in the suite in two ways, and may do either or both.

(a) Add its own area.  This is just "add a new area" above with the extension's
name: a prop_<ext>.c that defines PG_PROP_AREA and uses PG_PROP(), whose connect
helper runs CREATE EXTENSION IF NOT EXISTS <ext>.  Every contrib area already in
this tree (hstore, ltree, citext, cube, seg, intarray, pg_trgm, ...) is exactly
this shape.  An out-of-tree extension ships the file and arranges for it to be
compiled into the pg_prop binary.

(b) Augment an existing area through an extension point.  A core area may
publish a named point and call pg_prop_run_hooks() there; an extension registers
a contributor with PG_PROP_HOOK("point", fn).  The SQL query generator
(sql/query_gen.h) publishes three:

    query_gen.setup      run DDL (CREATE EXTENSION) before each generated table;
                         ctx is the live connection.
    query_gen.column     add a typed column to the generated table; ctx is the
                         qg_schema being assembled.
    query_gen.predicate  emit a predicate leaf over the extension's column; ctx
                         is a qg_pred_ctx (schema + buffer).

Registering those three teaches the generator about the extension's type, after
which every query-level oracle -- TLP, NoREC, cardinality, PQS, plan-advice --
generates tables and queries that use the type and checks all of its invariants
over it, with no new oracle code.  sql/prop_query_ext_citext.c is a complete
worked example (it also adds one citext-specific area, to show both styles in
one file).  A core area makes itself extensible simply by publishing a point and
documenting the ctx type its contributors receive.
